Affirmation is the owner's layer: a suggestion is forked into Affirmed and linked to it with derived-from. Earlier rulings keep that layer the owner's:
- decision 2800bea0: affirmed clusters are the owner's;
- the handbook's "Human forks only";
- RFC-046: createdBy is testimony, never authority;
- the Method editor's write guard keeps agents out of Affirmed.

In srs-programme, a human affirmed all 26 affirmed tensions. 17 of them still contain 34 suggested poles, none of which has an affirmed fork. The poles carry their own meaning: what leaning that way looks like (looks_like) and how it fails when overdone (shadow). A tension's poles become nested parts with the repair in srs-programme#536. The Method editor's one-act affirm (srs-web#581) will then fork a tension and its poles together, with both in view.

The owner calls this "a principle <> practical decision that we should make with care" (vignette 36bc6aae). It sits on the Versioning and Reference trade-off. Its affirmed principle b3ac3391 is "Semantic integrity over practical expression", with the boundary clause "Expression can be added as a layer… Meaning should always reside at the meaning layer."
